Exercise 1.20 starts from any neighbourhood system $\mathcal{D}$ over $\Delta$, sets $\Delta'=\mathcal{D}$ and $\mathcal{D}'=\{\uparrow X\mid X\in\mathcal{D}\}$ with $\uparrow X=\{Y\in\mathcal{D}\mid Y\subseteq X\}$, and asks to show that $\mathcal{D}'$ is positive and that $|\mathcal{D}|$ is isomorphic to $|\mathcal{D}'|$, noting that tokens and finite elements of $\mathcal{D}'$ are in one-one correspondence. `upSet X` is $\{Y\mid Y\in\mathcal{D}\land Y\subseteq X\}$. `upSet_inter` is $\uparrow X\cap\uparrow Y=\uparrow(X\cap Y)$, and `upSet_subset_iff` is $\uparrow X\subseteq\uparrow Y$ if and only if $X\subseteq Y$ for $X\in\mathcal{D}$, by testing $X\in\uparrow X$ and by transitivity of inclusion; `upSet_injective` is the two inclusions. `powerSystem` takes sets as tokens and declares a set of tokens to be a neighbourhood when it equals $\uparrow X$ for some $X\in\mathcal{D}$, with master $\uparrow\Delta$; a witness $Z\in\uparrow Z\subseteq\uparrow X\cap\uparrow Y$ gives $Z\subseteq X$ and $Z\subseteq Y$, so $X\cap Y\in\mathcal{D}$ and the intersection of the up-sets is $\uparrow(X\cap Y)$. `powerSystem_isPositive` says $\uparrow X\cap\uparrow Y$ is a neighbourhood of $\mathcal{D}'$ if and only if it is nonempty: an up-set equal to $\uparrow W$ contains $W$, and a neighbourhood contained in the intersection is a consistency witness that puts $X\cap Y$ into $\mathcal{D}$. `toPower` sends a filter $x$ to $\{\uparrow W\mid W\in x\}$, closing the image under intersection by $\uparrow W_1\cap\uparrow W_2=\uparrow(W_1\cap W_2)$ and under enlargement by `upSet_subset_iff`. `ofPower` sends a filter $y$ of $\mathcal{D}'$ to $\{W\in\mathcal{D}\mid\uparrow W\in y\}$. Its gloss says that a subset of such a neighbourhood is a neighbourhood; the field `sub` records that the member itself is a neighbourhood. The intersection clause of `ofPower` does use a genuine consistency witness: `y.sub` writes $\uparrow(X\cap Y)$ as $\uparrow W$, and $W\subseteq X\cap Y$ lets the original intersection law return $X\cap Y$. `powerIso` inverts the two maps by extensionality, recovering a neighbourhood from its up-set by `upSet_injective`, and it reflects filter inclusion in the same way. The right-inverse gloss says an up-set in the image is recovered from a consistency witness; the proof uses `y.sub`, that the member is the up-set of some original neighbourhood. `toPower_principal` sends the principal filter of $X$ to the principal filter of $\uparrow X$. Together with injectivity of $\uparrow$, that is the raw material of the note that tokens of $\mathcal{D}'$ correspond to finite elements, and the glosses never state the bijection. `example_120` is the isomorphism, positivity, and `toPower_principal`.

**Theorem:** partial — positivity of $\mathcal{D}'$ and the order isomorphism $|\mathcal{D}|\cong|\mathcal{D}'|$ are `powerSystem_isPositive` and `powerIso`, while the one-one correspondence of tokens of $\mathcal{D}'$ with finite elements is not stated. **Proof:** partial — the up-set intersection law and the two inverse maps match those two claims; the `ofPower` gloss misstates `sub`, and the `powerIso` gloss misnames the witness used on the right inverse.
